{"name":"Silver Creek","hex":"#d9dad2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Silver Creek","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A number of streams and creeks in the United States and Canada, and probably elsewhere:\nA left tributary of the Little Colorado, Arizona, United States.","A left tributary of the Little Colorado, Arizona, United States."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Silver_Creek"}],"entries":[{"title":"Silver Creek, Queensland","blocks":[{"text":"Silver Creek is a rural locality in the Whitsunday Region, Queensland, Australia. In the 2021 census, Silver Creek had a population of 18 people."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Creek%2C_Queensland"},{"title":"Silver Creek, Belize","blocks":[{"text":"Silver Creek is a village in the Toledo District of Belize. According to the 2000 census, Silver Creek had a population of 1,326 people. There is also a stream named Silver Creek at this same location. Near the village of Silver Creek is an ancient Maya civilization site. Nearby there is a larger Maya site from at least as early as 700 AD, Lubaantun."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Creek%2C_Belize"},{"title":"Silver Creek (Susquehanna River tributary)","blocks":[{"text":"Silver Creek is a tributary of the Susquehanna River in Snyder County, Pennsylvania, in the United States. It is approximately 6.1 miles (9.8 km) long and flows through Washington Township and Union Township. The watershed of the creek has an area of 5.92 square miles (15.3 km2). The creek has no named tributaries, but it does have several unnamed tributaries. Many of these, as well as Silver Creek itself, are impaired by sedimentation/siltation due to grazing-related agriculture. The watershed is partly or fully in the Ridge and Valley physiographic province."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver_Creek_(Susquehanna_River_tributary)"},{"title":"Silver","blocks":[{"text":"Silver is a chemical element; it has symbol Ag and atomic number 47. A soft, white, lustrous transition metal, it exhibits the highest electrical conductivity, thermal conductivity and reflectivity of any metal. Silver is found in the Earth's crust in the pure, free elemental form, as an alloy with gold (electrum) and other metals, and in minerals such as acanthite, argentite and chlorargyrite. Most silver is recovered as a byproduct of lead–zinc, copper and gold mining and refining."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Silver_crystal.jpg/330px-Silver_crystal.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Silver"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg/500px-1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg/500px-1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Silver bullion bar, 1000 ounces","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A1000oz.silver.bullion.bar.top.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Ag_atomic_wire.jpg/500px-Ag_atomic_wire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Ag_atomic_wire.jpg/500px-Ag_atomic_wire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Silver is extremely ductile and, like gold, can be drawn into a wire one atom wide.[14]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAg_atomic_wire.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Sulfid_st%C5%99%C3%ADbrn%C3%BD.PNG/500px-Sulfid_st%C5%99%C3%ADbrn%C3%BD.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://t